Q1. What is a PL/SQL Block?

Interview Ready Answer

A PL/SQL block is the basic executable unit of a PL/SQL program.
It is a logical container that groups SQL statements and procedural
statements into a single executable block.

Key Points

• Basic executable unit of PL/SQL.
• Contains SQL and procedural statements.
• Executes as a single unit.
• Supports modular programming.
• Can be anonymous or named.

======================================================================

Q2. What are the sections of a PL/SQL Block?

Interview Ready Answer

A PL/SQL block consists of four sections.

1. DECLARE
2. BEGIN
3. EXCEPTION
4. END

Each section has a specific purpose.

Structure

DECLARE
    -- Variable declarations

BEGIN
    -- Executable statements

EXCEPTION
    -- Exception handling

END;

======================================================================

Q3. What is the purpose of the DECLARE section?

Interview Ready Answer

The DECLARE section is used to declare local variables, constants,
cursors and user-defined data types required by the PL/SQL block.

Key Points

• Used for variable declaration.
• Used for constants.
• Used for cursors.
• Used for user-defined data types.
• Variables can also be initialized here.

Important

DECLARE section is OPTIONAL.

======================================================================

Q4. What is the purpose of the BEGIN section?

Interview Ready Answer

The BEGIN section contains the executable statements of the PL/SQL
program. It is the main section where business logic is written.

Key Points

• Contains executable statements.
• SQL statements are written here.
• IF-ELSE statements.
• Loops.
• Function calls.
• Procedure calls.

Important

BEGIN section is MANDATORY.

======================================================================

Q5. What is the purpose of the EXCEPTION section?

Interview Ready Answer

The EXCEPTION section is used to handle runtime errors that occur
during the execution of the BEGIN section.

Key Points

• Handles runtime exceptions.
• Prevents abnormal program termination.
• Improves reliability.
• Executes only when an exception occurs.

Important

EXCEPTION section is OPTIONAL.

======================================================================

Q6. What is the purpose of the END section?

Interview Ready Answer

The END section marks the end of a PL/SQL block.

Key Points

• Terminates the PL/SQL block.
• Every PL/SQL block must end with END;
• END is always followed by a semicolon.

Important

END section is MANDATORY.

======================================================================

Q7. Which sections of a PL/SQL block are mandatory and which are
optional?

Interview Ready Answer

Mandatory Sections

• BEGIN
• END

Optional Sections

• DECLARE
• EXCEPTION

Memory Trick

DECLARE
↓

BEGIN
↓

EXCEPTION
↓

END

Only BEGIN and END are compulsory.

======================================================================

Q8. Can a PL/SQL block exist without a DECLARE section?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

A PL/SQL block can exist without a DECLARE section because DECLARE
is optional.

If the program does not require variables, constants, cursors or
user-defined data types, the DECLARE section can be omitted.

Example

BEGIN

    NULL;

END;

======================================================================

Q9. Why is the BEGIN section mandatory?

Interview Ready Answer

The BEGIN section is mandatory because it contains the executable
statements of the PL/SQL block.

Without the BEGIN section, there is no executable code and the
program cannot run.

======================================================================

Q10. Types of PL/SQL Blocks

Interview Ready Answer

PL/SQL blocks are of two types.

1. Anonymous Block

• Does not have a name.
• Executes only once.
• Cannot be stored in the database.

2. Named Block

• Has a name.
• Stored in the database.
• Can be executed multiple times.
• Examples:
  • Procedure
  • Function
  • Package
  • Trigger

